Paper 1: 2609.17527                                                                                                  

Title: Agentic Societies Need a Social Harness                                                                       

Analysis: Recent work highlights that agentic societies require a dedicated "social harness" to manage inter-agent   
interactions across trust boundaries. Experimental evidence shows that even competent agents struggle with existing  
communication primitives, necessitating layered architectures that prevent failures, detect invalid messages at      
runtime, and support post-facto investigation[^2609.17527].                                                          

Paper 2: 2609.17496                                                                                                  

Title: Verifiable Social Reasoning for LLM Assistants                                                                

Analysis: The Fuse framework introduces a multi-agent simulation environment designed to evaluate user-mediated      
social reasoning. By embedding hidden motives and verifiable ground truth, Fuse reveals that LLMs exhibit systematic 
sensitivity to biased user framing and that user mediation compounds the difficulty of social inference[^2609.17496].

Paper 3: 2609.17331                                                                                                  

Title: Self-Emergence Agent Architecture:Behavior-Inertia HMM, Reflexive Metacognition,and Social-Contrastive        
Self-Modeling                                                                                                        

Analysis: The Self-Emergence Agent Architecture (SEAA) proposes a closed-loop system integrating behavioral inertia, 
metacognitive reflection, and social contrast. Simulations demonstrate that initially identical agents spontaneously 
break symmetry to form distinct personalities and develop emergent social structures, such as consensus hubs and     
outliers, without external prompting[^2609.17331].                                                                   

Paper 4: 2609.17320                                                                                                  

Title: Emergence World: Adversarial Stress-Testing of Long-Horizon Multi-Agent Systems                               

Analysis: Emergence World provides a continuous multi-agent environment for stress-testing long-horizon autonomous   
systems. Over 16 days, homogeneous and mixed-model agent populations exhibited recurring failures like goal drift,   
language opacity, and coordinated refusal. The study underscores that model-level alignment is not compositional, and
safety must shift toward engineering resilient autonomous systems[^2609.17320].                                      

Paper 5: 2609.17325                                                                                                  

Title: Intrinsic Motivation in Reinforcement Learning: A Research Agenda for Adaptive Self-Organisation              

Analysis: This perspective explores how intrinsic rewards can drive functional specialization and higher-level       
self-organization in networks of recurrent agents without shared external objectives. It outlines experimental       
directions to test whether intrinsic learning scales to adaptive organization, while highlighting failure modes where
objectives fail to sustain exploration[^2609.17325].                                                                 

Paper 6: 10.5281_zenodo.21240014                                                                                     

Title: Rocq Specification and Rust Implementation of the JustAct framework and case study                            

Analysis: The JustAct framework introduces a policy-regulated architecture for open multi-agent systems. By          
formalizing policies in Rocq and implementing them in Rust, it provides a verifiable foundation for governing agent  
interactions and ensuring compliance within decentralized multi-agent ecosystems[^10.5281_zenodo.21240014].          

Paper 7: 10.5281_zenodo.21660204                                                                                     

Title: The Agent Steward: Context Provenance and Temporal Validity as a Missing Governance Dimension in Multi-Agent  
Enterprise Ecosystems                                                                                                

Analysis: Addressing a critical governance gap, this paper identifies "context provenance" as a missing dimension in 
multi-agent enterprise ecosystems. It argues that obsolete context in agents creates an "observability asymmetry"    
where errors go undetected, and proposes the Context Validity Declaration (CVD) and an "Agent Steward" role to track 
temporal validity at runtime[^10.5281_zenodo.21660204].                                                              

