Let's check 10.5281_zenodo.21660204 abstract: "Enterprise adoption of AI agents has produced a governance failure    
mode that current frameworks do not address. When individual business domains independently build agents, each agent 
must be provisioned with organizational context \u2014 product structure, customer profiles, business rules, internal
policy. Absent a standard mechanism for exposing this context, it is replicated: copied, adapted, and embedded into  
each agent. Replication creates a liability that manifests only over time, one that is nearly impossible to measure  
and to govern. When organizational reality changes, the copies do not change together. This paper argues that the    
resulting failure is categorically different from its well-studied ancestor in data management, and that the         
difference is what makes it dangerous. Obsolete data artifacts preserve observable markers of obsolescence \u2014    
stale timestamps, broken references, schema drift. Obsolete agent outputs preserve none. An agent operating on       
six-month-old context and an agent operating on current context produce responses that are indistinguishable in form,
tone, and expressed confidence. We term this the observability asymmetry: obsolescence lost its observable signature 
when it moved from the data layer to the natural language layer. We situate this problem against current work. Recent
gap analysis of agent interoperability protocols (Kang & Diponegoro, 2026) establishes that governance constitutes a 
missing architectural layer above MCP, A2A, ACP, ANP and ERC-8004, deriving a six-dimension taxonomy covering        
membership, deliberation, voting, dissent preservation, human escalation, and audit. We observe that all six         
dimensions address the decision process, and none addresses the epistemic status of the knowledge informing the      
decision. A perfectly auditable deliberation, with recorded votes and preserved dissent, can reach a wrong conclusion
because every participant operated on expired context \u2014 and the existing taxonomy cannot detect this. We propose
that context provenance and temporal validity constitute a governance dimension orthogonal to existing taxonomies. We
contribute three artifacts: (1) the accountability triad, formalizing the Agent Steward alongside the Agent Owner and
Agent Custodian roles, distinguished by the question each answers; (2) the Context Validity Declaration (CVD), a     
minimal set of five declarative primitives making obsolescence detectable at runtime; and (3) the silent error rate  
as an evaluation metric for multi-agent ecosystems, shifting assessment from "did the agent err?" to "did the agent  
err without signaling?". This is a position paper. The artifacts are specified but not yet empirically validated; a  
validation design is proposed."                                                                                      

Markdown: "Additionally, the "Agent Steward" concept identifies context provenance as a missing governance dimension,
proposing runtime temporal validity declarations to mitigate the "observability asymmetry" where agents operate on   
obsolete context without signaling errors[^10.5281_zenodo.21660204]."                                                

Matches.                                                                                                             

